# broiler-js-conformance test262 floor 1
# The whole-suite --test262 ratchet. A later run of THIS revision under THIS
# manifest may not cross a row below. `atLeast` may not fall; `atMost` may not
# rise. Exhaustions are deliberately not held: a spent allowance says what the
# machine was doing, not what the engine does. A revision or manifest change
# re-bases with --admit and retires the old rows here with their reason.
#
# RE-BASED BY HAND ON 2026-09-08, AND THE FACT THAT --admit COULD NOT DO IT IS
# THE POINT. `--admit` re-bases only when the suite revision or the manifest
# moved; neither moved here, so the ratchet answered `Regressed` and refused,
# which is the design working. What moved is the ENGINE, deliberately: the wide
# front end had been admitting a BigInt literal and evaluating it as a Number,
# so `9007199254740993n` answered `9007199254740992`, and the refusal it should
# have met was restored in JsParser.cs. Sixty variants of
# test/language/literals/bigint alone moved from a mixture of accidental passes
# and silent failures onto one named refusal, and 1,990 across the suite.
#
# THE FLOOR IS THEREFORE LOWER ON `passed` AND THAT IS NOT A REGRESSION, which
# is exactly the claim a ratchet exists to stop anybody making casually - so it
# is made here, in writing, with the retired rows kept below rather than
# deleted. 319 variants that had been passing by accident stopped passing;
# 1,671 that had been failing silently now meet a refusal naming the construct.
# The profile's rule is that a refusal is a supported answer and a plausible
# wrong value is not (roadmap.parity.md section 1), so a smaller honest total
# replaced a larger dishonest one. JSC-207 carries the finding.
#
# A reader who wants to move `passed` back up should widen the manifest to
# implement BigInt, not narrow the refusal.
suite test262
